1
00:00:00,420 --> 00:00:08,327
Every triangle has thousands of hidden centers, and the Encyclopedia of Triangle Centers begins right here at X one: the Incenter.

2
00:00:08,327 --> 00:00:18,850
Join the three interior angle bisectors of triangle A B C, and they concur at a single invariant point, the center of the unique inscribed circle tangent to all three sides.

3
00:00:19,270 --> 00:00:26,957
Unlike a sequential proof in Euclid, a triangle center is defined by its homogeneous coordinates, which have two direct geometric pictures.

4
00:00:26,957 --> 00:00:30,330
First, look at the Trilinear coordinates, alpha, beta, gamma.

5
00:00:30,330 --> 00:00:33,759
Drop perpendiculars from X one to sidelines B C, C A, and A B.

6
00:00:33,759 --> 00:00:43,160
Because all three perpendicular struts are radii of the same incircle, their lengths are identical, giving X one the simplest trilinear ratio of all: one, to one, to one.

7
00:00:43,580 --> 00:00:46,667
Second, look at the Barycentric coordinates, u, v, w.

8
00:00:46,667 --> 00:00:52,141
Join X one to vertices A, B, and C, partitioning the triangle into three shaded sub-triangles.

9
00:00:52,141 --> 00:01:00,004
Each sub-triangle has base a, b, or c, and the same height r, so their areas are in the exact ratio of the side lengths: a, to b, to c.

10
00:01:00,004 --> 00:01:17,359
Compare the three classic centers side by side: X one has equal perpendicular distances, one to one to one; the centroid X two has equal sub-triangle areas, one to one to one; and the symmedian point X six has distances proportional to the sides, and areas proportional to the squares of the sides.

11
00:01:17,359 --> 00:01:23,940
Across acute, right, Kepler, and Kimberling triangles, X one remains the invariant heart of the inscribed circle.
